The `etcd1` member started a read that was assigned index 10, while 460ms earlier `etcd2` has commited index 11. This is breaking linearizability as you cannot reorder requests that didn't intersect. 

Logs also include failures injected by Anththesis. Looks like we have combination of network partition and container pausing.  Pausing is not something that we tested in robustness test.

This is how it looks on visualization (after removing failed requests and patching): 

<img width="1151" height="332" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/9e80eacf-ecc9-407c-bc8e-1334ca5098df" />

Report:
[var_report_dump (3).tar.gz](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/25516086/var_report_dump.3.tar.gz)


Next step: instrument the read index loop for client server interaction. 

EDIT: Fixed 90ms -> 460ms

